Main Content\
\
#1. Safety and scope preface\
- The README\'92s claims are allegation-only and non-corroborated. This kit does not verify them.\
- The DELIBERATE document is treated as an allegation-only case study of alleged privacy-floor violations.\
- The HUMINT manual is used for ethical and analytical principles only: consent, humane treatment, oversight, screening, source/subject protection, and epistemic humility. Its interrogation approaches are not role-played as coercion; where mentioned, they are discussed as *why transparency and consent matter*.\
- Do not simulate: sexual humiliation, threats, law-enforcement impersonation, medical/surgical procedures, covert surveillance of non-consenting people, or any actual interrogation for information.\
- Do simulate: the *weight* of privacy loss under consent, with a stop phrase and debrief.\
\
#2. Reflection on the two attached documents\
\
What FM 2-22.3 teaches that matters here\
- Legal and humane constraints are central. The manual repeatedly requires compliance with law and policy, prohibits torture and cruel/inhuman/degrading treatment, and requires humane treatment of all detainees. That is the same floor this kit teaches: *no one should be subjected to non-consensual, inescapable treatment.*\
- Oversight and accountability. The manual emphasizes command responsibility, supervision, approval chains, legal review, and reporting. Translated to this kit: consent ledgers, audit trails, and a named mediator.\
- Screening and source assessment. The manual distinguishes screening from exploitation and stresses evaluating knowledge, cooperation, and reliability. Translated: teach participants to separate *observation* from *inference* from *access*.\
- Elicitation and rapport. The manual describes elicitation as conversation where the source may not know the purpose. That is precisely why transparency and consent are ethical prerequisites in any simulation; without them, \'93elicitation\'94 becomes manipulation.\
- Deception detection and epistemic humility. The manual lists inconsistencies, body-language mismatches, and self-serving information as *indicators*, not proof. Translated: the observer-error tracker teaches that inference is fallible.\
- Protection of sources and subjects. The manual stresses source protection and OPSEC. Translated: the simulation protects participants\'92 data (local-only, no recording) and never asks them to disclose real private information.\
\
What the DELIBERATE document alleges as failure modes\
- Concealment without transparency; no accessible off switch; external control; continuous operation; low-latency closed-loop commentary; titration to avoid rejection; escalation into sexual/embarrassment content; OODA-loop automation with human-in-the-loop steering; use of voice recreation; sleep disruption; and the alleged targeting of inner speech/planned speech.\
- These are used only as a checklist of *what not to do* and *what to teach against*: consent, revocability, control, transparency, silence, and independent review.\
\
#3. Principles distilled\
\
| Principle | From HUMINT doctrine | From the alleged case | Simulation rule |\
|---|---|---|---|\
| Consent | Legal/humane constraints; approval chains | Alleged absence of informed consent | Written, voluntary, revocable consent gate |\
| Transparency | Reporting and oversight | Alleged concealment | Purpose, roles, and limits stated up front |\
| Control | Command responsibility | Alleged inaccessible controller | Subject holds the stop phrase and can end any module |\
| Revocability | Termination phase | Alleged no off switch | Stop means stop; no follow-up pressure |\
| Humane treatment | Prohibition of torture/CIDT | Alleged continuous no-reprieve stimulation | No threats, no sexual content, no impersonation |\
| Epistemic humility | Screening and deception indicators | Alleged \'93decoding\'94 certainty | Label observation vs. inference vs. access |\
| Source/subject protection | Source protection, OPSEC | Alleged device/telemetry access | Local-only data; no recording; no real secrets |\
| Auditability | Reporting architecture | Alleged hidden controller/software | Consent ledger, facilitator notes, debrief export |\
| Silence | Termination and breaks | Alleged 24/7 no silence | Built-in silence and rest periods |\
\
#4. Refined simulation modules\
\
Each module includes: Purpose \'b7 Setup \'b7 Script \'b7 Stop \'b7 Debrief \'b7 Doctrine note \'b7 Contrast note.\
\
##Module 1 \'97 The 30-Minute Commentary Chair (zero cost)\
- Purpose: distinguish occasional observation from continuous commentary.\
- Setup: consenting subject does a harmless task (sort cards, draft a grocery list). Consented observer comments only on *observable* behavior, every 30 minutes or on a timer. No comments on thoughts, body, sexuality, or identity.\
- Script: \'93You paused.\'94 \'93You opened a new tab.\'94 \'93You looked away.\'94\
- Stop: subject says \'93pause\'94; everything stops.\
- Debrief: What felt like privacy? What felt like surveillance? What changed with frequency?\
- Doctrine note: the manual separates screening from exploitation; this module shows the difference between observing and exploiting.\
- Contrast note: the alleged case describes continuous commentary with no silence; this module makes the *frequency* feel tangible without replicating harm.\
\
##Module 2 \'97 Inner-Speech Guessing Game (zero cost)\
- Purpose: teach inference limits.\
- Setup: subject silently writes a sentence or chooses a category (errand, memory, worry). Observer guesses only the *category*, not the content.\
- Script: \'93I think you were thinking about an errand.\'94 Record hits and misses.\
- Stop: either party can stop.\
- Debrief: Most guesses will be weak. This is the accuracy lesson: observed behavior is not decoded inner speech.\
- Doctrine note: the manual treats deception indicators as probabilistic, not proof.\
- Contrast note: the alleged case claims real-time decoding; this module shows how *context* can create a false sense of access.\
\
##Module 3 \'97 The No-Off-Switch Ten Minutes (zero to low cost)\
- Purpose: demonstrate the ethical weight of inaccessibility.\
- Setup: with consent, subject wears headphones playing a neutral recorded voice reading a consent script or describing the room. The subject cannot adjust volume for ten minutes. Mediator holds the stop phrase.\
- Script: neutral, non-persuasive, non-sexual, non-threatening content.\
- Stop: mediator or subject stops immediately.\
- Debrief: contrast a consensual ten-minute exercise with an alleged 24/7 inability to disable stimulation. Ask: what did lack of control feel like?\
- Doctrine note: the manual requires humane treatment and prohibits coercion; this module is the *consented* inverse.\
- Contrast note: the alleged case describes no accessible off switch; this module makes the *absence of control* the lesson, not the content.\
\
##Module 4 \'97 Closed-Loop Observer/AI Demo (low to <$1,000)\
- Purpose: show how responsive commentary can *feel* like decoding without being decoding.\
- Setup: consented subject; local speech-to-text or human observer; comments only on visible activity on a shared screen or table. Optional neutral TTS.\
- Script: \'93This system sees observable activity and hears consented speech; it does not read thoughts.\'94\
- Stop: subject stops; observer stops.\
- Debrief: discuss latency, context, and why responsiveness can create a false sense of total access.\
- Doctrine note: the manual\'92s \'93we know all\'94 and \'93file and dossier\'94 approaches are *ruses*; here we label the ruse and debrief it.\
- Contrast note: the alleged case describes a closed loop with AI and human-in-the-loop; this module shows the *feeling* without the violation.\
\
##Module 5 \'97 Eyes-Closed Attention Exercise (zero cost)\
- Purpose: show how removing visual anchors changes perceived privacy.\
- Setup: subject sits with eyes closed or a blindfold they choose; mirrors covered. A neutral voice reads a grounding script every few minutes.\
- Script: grounding, not persuasion; no commentary on the body, sexuality, or identity.\
- Stop: subject stops at any time.\
- Debrief: what changed in attention, anxiety, and sense of control?\
- Doctrine note: the manual stresses control and rapport; here the subject holds control.\
- Contrast note: the alleged case describes eyes-closed, mirror-covered persuasion; this module replaces persuasion with grounding.\
\
##Module 6 \'97 Consent & Control Audit (new; zero cost)\
- Purpose: make power asymmetry visible.\
- Setup: draw the power map: Subject, Observer, Controller, Software, AI, Data Store, Off Switch. Circle who holds the off switch.\
- Script: \'93Who can stop it? Who knows what is stored? Who can revoke?\'94\
- Debrief: compare with the alleged case: inaccessible controller, external software, no off switch.\
- Doctrine note: the manual\'92s approval chains and technical control translate to visible authority.\
- Contrast note: the alleged case\'92s \'93controller outside my access\'94 is the core failure this audit surfaces.\
\
##Module 7 \'97 Titration Awareness Exercise (new; zero cost)\
- Purpose: teach how gradual escalation avoids rejection.\
- Setup: facilitator reads a short, neutral story of a *hypothetical* system that starts with mild comments and slowly increases frequency. Participants mark where they would have said \'93stop.\'94\
- Script: no actual harassment; only a written timeline.\
- Debrief: why is gradual escalation hard to notice? What safeguards (consent gates, check-ins, independent review) catch it early?\
- Doctrine note: the manual\'92s approach phase stresses rapport and gradual transitions; the ethical version makes transitions *visible and consensual*.\
- Contrast note: the DELIBERATE document alleges titration to avoid rejection; this exercise teaches *detection and refusal*.\
\
#5. Mental exercises and interactives\
\
1. Consent ledger: write every data flow in the demo\'97who sees what, who stores it, who can stop it. Compare with the alleged case\'92s \'93activity out, audio in, controller inaccessible.\'94\
2. Privacy-floor scale: before/after, rate 0\'9610: \'93I could stop it,\'94 \'93I knew who was observing,\'94 \'93I knew what they knew,\'94 \'93I had silence.\'94 Discuss gaps.\
3. Observer error audit: compute the error rate in the guessing game. Use it to challenge \'93they must be reading my mind\'94 with \'93they may be guessing from context.\'94\
4. Power map: draw the nodes and circle the off switch. This is often the clearest teaching image.\
5. Grounding protocol: 5-4-3-2-1 sensory grounding, slow breathing, trusted-person check-in.\
6. Debrief triad: \'93What did you notice? What did you feel? What will you do differently?\'94 Keep it non-diagnostic.\
7. Doctrine-to-demo mapping: match each HUMINT principle (consent, oversight, screening, termination) to a demo rule. This makes the ethics explicit.\
\
#6. Mediator protocol \'97 \'93Consent\'96Scope\'96Stop\'96Debrief\'94\
\
1. Consent: written, verbal, voluntary, revocable. No recording, no deception, no real personal data.\
2. Scope: state exactly what will be simulated and what will not. Exclude sexual content, threats, law enforcement, medical procedures.\
3. Roles: subject, observer, mediator, note-taker. The mediator can stop at any time.\
4. Stop signal: a single word stops everything. No questions asked until debrief.\
5. Monitoring: watch for distress; offer a break; end early if needed.\
6. Debrief: separate fact from simulation; discuss privacy implications; offer grounding and follow-up.\
7. No diagnosis: the demo is educational, not clinical assessment. If real concerns persist, recommend independent medical, technical, and legal review.\
8. Facilitator-mode script: read the purpose, the stop phrase, and the \'93do not simulate\'94 list before every module.\
\
#7. Cost tiers\
\
- Zero cost: paper, timer, consent sheet, consented partner, shared task, blindfold, stop phrase.\
- Low cost (<$100): kitchen timer, white-noise machine, cheap USB mic, open-source audio editor, printed facilitator guide.\
- <$1,000: laptop with local transcription, scripted TTS, simple timer app, basic headphones, small speaker, optional consumer HR/GSR sensor for arousal feedback\'97clearly not thought-reading.\
- Any cost: professional facilitator, ethics review, secure room, spatial-audio setup, consumer EEG for \'93signals are noisy and not inner speech\'94 demonstrations, independent audit.\
\
#8. Major elective implantation as a hypothetical final resort\
\
If anyone ever proposes an elective implant for this kind of concern, the burden should be extreme: independent medical second opinion, neurotology and audiology review, ethics committee, reversible design, accessible off switch, no concealment, data-minimization plan, security audit, hearing-preservation plan for musicians, legal counsel, waiting period, and ongoing consent. A concealed, non-revocable, wirelessly controlled device is a red flag, not a solution. The alleged case is the cautionary tale: no transparency, no accessible control, no revocability, no silence. The ethical alternative is consent, control, and independent oversight\'97or no device at all.\
\
#9. Debrief and follow-up\
\
- Immediate: grounding, stop phrase confirmed, consent ledger reviewed, sliders compared.\
- Short-term: written reflection, facilitator notes, optional export.\
- If distress: trusted-person check-in, grounding, and a recommendation for independent medical/technical/legal review. This kit does not diagnose.\
- If the participant is the README author or someone with similar concerns: preserve records, seek independent forensic and medical review, and keep any evaluation hearing-protective and non-coercive. The kit\'92s role is educational, not investigative.\
