BOOKEND B \'97 Acceptance Criteria and Facilitator Handoff\
\
#Acceptance criteria for the HTML build\
- Safety: allegation-only banner visible on every tab; \'93do not simulate\'94 list in facilitator mode; stop phrase always reachable.\
- Accessibility: passes automated checks (axe/Lighthouse) and manual keyboard/screen-reader review; reduced-motion respected.\
- Consent: cannot start any module until the consent gate is complete; stop button ends the module immediately.\
- Data: no network calls; export is local-only; no recording.\
- Content: every module has Purpose, Setup, Script, Stop, Debrief, Doctrine note, Contrast note.\
- Facilitator mode: toggles script, timing cues, and debrief prompts; does not reveal them to participants by default.\
- Print: consent sheet, facilitator script, and debrief sheet print cleanly.\
\
#Test plan\
1. Keyboard-only walkthrough of all tabs and modules.\
2. Screen-reader pass on consent gate, timers, sliders, and live regions.\
3. Contrast check on dark and light themes.\
4. Reduced-motion check (no animations, timers still functional).\
5. Stop-phrase test in every module.\
6. Export/print test with sample data.\
7. Facilitator dry-run with a consenting colleague.\
\
#Facilitator handoff\
- Read the safety preface aloud.\
- Confirm consent gate.\
- Run one module at a time; debrief before the next.\
- Use the power map and observer-error tracker as the core teaching images.\
- End with grounding and the debrief triad.\
- Remind participants: this is a simulation of the *weight* of privacy loss, not a replication of harm; the alleged case remains unverified and the ethical response is consent, control, transparency, and independent review.\
\
Bottom line: Use the two attached documents as allegation-only, principle-level case studies. From the HUMINT manual, take consent, humane treatment, oversight, screening, and epistemic humility. From the DELIBERATE document, take the failure-mode checklist: concealment, inaccessibility, no off switch, continuous operation, titration, escalation, and closed-loop manipulation. Build the demo so participants feel the *weight* of those failures without anyone being surveilled, implanted, impersonated, or harassed.}